Z||000001||Kasdan||10-10-2001||08:42 AM||||A tech 4 couldnt handle more than a 50hp increase without a significant rebuild....and even at that id still have it rebuilt, those engines wernt meant to be modified, they were the econ motors.  Sure, you can get power from them, but not safely.<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero21.jpg">          <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pheagey||10-10-2001||08:58 AM||||I must agree with Kasdan. More than 30-50hp gain in a Iron Duke w/o a rebuild is asking for a problem. My self Im saving up for a new lifters, headers, cam/crankshaft, etc. Gatta have an engine that can handle it, the Iron Duke 'can' handle it, just ganna get it built right.<BR> <A HREF="Http://www.LithuimEffects.com/car.html" TARGET=_blank>Http://www.LithuimEffects.com/car.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||erikred||10-10-2001||10:12 AM||||How long do you want it to last? <P>As with any engine, a large increase in power will require heavy duty parts inorder for it to last a long time. The "Iron Duke" engine only has about 90hp from the factory. Just to get to 180hp would be 100% gain. How many engines do you see that can handle a 100% increase in power without having to be beefed up.<P>There are many things you can do to get around 30-50 hp from the duke and it should be able to handle the power. However, depending on your knowledge of engines and pocket book, you may be better off to swap the engine and computer for a 3.4l v6. That is probably the most affordable swap out there. But it is still not cheap. Do a search through the tech. archives on this board to find out all about the different swaps and prices.<P>Back to the Duke. I do not believe anyone sells a turbo kit. However, a met a fellow who used a turbo off of a 2.3l Ford along with a spark control box and a Kennbell Boost-a-pump. There were no other mods to the engine. He estimated well over 200hp according to his quarter mile times. Thats not bad at all considering it can be done for under $1000. He said he did not experience any reliability problems with the engine, but don't expect it to last as long as a stock one. You could tune down the boost for street use and crank it up for racing to help it last longer.<P>Another option is NOS. This would be the easiest way to boost the power a bunch. It is the same as the turbo though. Go mild with it or the motor probaly will not last long. A 50hp bost from NOS should be ok as long as you keep the revs below about 5200RPM.<P>There are also many performance parts still available for the Iron Duke engine. A header, the right cam, and some tuning should get you close to 120hp. It may not seem like much, but 120hp is a 1/3 increase. The car will definatly seem better.<P>If you did a complete HiPo rebuild, you can get over 300hp! But, it is expensive to do so. Kansas Racing Products (there site is down right now) sells the Super Duty version of these engines, which is what you would need to handle 300hp. Check out this qoute from their brochure.<P>"The KRP engines have a wide range of costs.  The lower budget racer can run one of our fully-machined engine blocks utilizing a small block Ford head, reworked by the racer, using the conventional Pontiac water pump, Pontiac internal oil pump, a stock oil pan, along with our high performance intake manifold for less than $5,000.  The engine would include high compression 350 cubic inch pistons and a Pontiac camshaft.  The crankshaft would be a KRP nodular iron crank developed specifically for racing.  Standard strokes available are 3.000, 3.100, or 3.250.  An engine in that configuration generates a tremendous amount of horsepower through parts that are readily available aftermarket and can still be a very reliable engine."<P>So it all depends on what you want, and how much money you have. BTW, I am doing a high performance rebuild on my 4 cyl. I will be running a turbo. The projected cost is $1500 including all machine work and parts. Horsepower should be around 250 with 10psi of boost.<P>Erik-<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000004||Master_Sushi||10-10-2001||11:18 AM||||I might try like a 50HP shot of nos on my 84 winter beater. It would be kind of fun. Even if it blows the engine I wont really care. With the 4.10 gears and a 50 HP nos it should be fast. I dont know how long it would last though, 84s had the weakest engines and I think my HG is not in the best of shape... Maybe ill try it towards the end of winter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||14
